u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"facsimile-images\">\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"apparatus\">\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>  \u003Cspan>Text after Cordano 1992\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"translation\">\u003Ch2>English translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">  \u003C/p>\u003Cp>  \u003C/p>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"translation\">\u003Ch2>Italian translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">  \u003C/p>\u003Cp>  \u003C/p>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"commentary\">\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>This document is one of a large deposit (c. 150) of lead tesserae found in a storeroom of Athena's sanctuary at the archaeological site of Kamarina. The lamina appears to be used twice (see Cordano 1992, p. 58). In the second use, face a is used as face b and vice versa. The first text has not been erased in order to engrave the second, therefore it is clearly visible. For a similar reuse of the tesserae see also \u003Ca href=\"http://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/inscription/ISic030197\" target=\"_blank\">ISic030197\u003C/a> and \u003Ca href=\"http://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/inscription/ISic030198\" target=\"_blank\">ISic030198\u003C/a>.\u003C/p>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"bibliography\">\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Cordano 1992 82b \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002437\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002437\u003C/a>\u003C/li>\u003Cli> 1992 42.0846 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10000796\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10000796\u003C/a>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> 2002 52.0920 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10000796\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10000796\u003C/a>\u003C/li>\u003Cli> 2004 54.0892 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/